# n8n Custom Node Development

##### The eleventh copy of the same HTTP request

An internal platform team supported thirty-odd workflows that all talked to the company's inventory service. Every one used an HTTP Request node with the same base URL, the same header construction, the same pagination loop copy-pasted from the last workflow, and the same brittle assumption about the error format.

When the service moved to v3 and changed its auth header, the migration took two weeks. Nobody could enumerate which workflows were affected without opening all of them.

A **custom node** solves this structurally. One package, one credential type, one implementation of pagination and error handling — and thirty workflows that update when you publish a new version. It also transforms the developer experience for everyone else: instead of constructing requests, colleagues drag in an "Inventory" node and pick an operation from a dropdown.

Extensible plugin architectures exist precisely for this reason, and n8n's is unusually approachable because the common case requires almost no imperative code.

##### Declarative versus programmatic

n8n offers two node styles, and choosing correctly is the assignment's central question.

**Declarative** nodes describe the HTTP request in a `routing` property. You write no request code at all — you declare the URL, method, query parameters and body mappings, and n8n's engine builds and sends the request, handles the response, and manages errors. Less code, fewer bugs, better performance in queue mode, and easier to review.

**Programmatic** nodes implement an `execute()` method and do everything themselves. Full control over the request lifecycle, arbitrary logic between calls, custom pagination, non-HTTP protocols, binary handling.

The selection rule: **use declarative for straightforward REST APIs — which is most of them — and programmatic only when you genuinely need imperative control.** Concretely, reach for programmatic when you must chain dependent requests, implement non-standard pagination, transform binary data, connect over a non-HTTP protocol (a database driver, a message queue), or implement a trigger node that polls with custom state.

A useful middle path: declarative nodes support `preSend` and `postReceive` hooks, which let you inject small amounts of logic — signing a request, unwrapping a response envelope — without abandoning the declarative model entirely.

##### Scaffolding and structure

```bash
git clone https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n-nodes-starter.git n8n-nodes-inventory
cd n8n-nodes-inventory
npm install
```

The layout that matters:

```text
n8n-nodes-inventory/
├── credentials/
│   └── InventoryApi.credentials.ts
├── nodes/
│   └── Inventory/
│       ├── Inventory.node.ts
│       ├── InventoryDescription.ts      # operations & fields (keeps the node file small)
│       └── inventory.svg                # node icon
├── package.json                         # the n8n section registers everything
└── tsconfig.json
```

`package.json` is what n8n reads to discover your node:

```json
{
  "name": "n8n-nodes-inventory",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "keywords": ["n8n-community-node-package"],
  "n8n": {
    "n8nNodesApiVersion": 1,
    "credentials": ["dist/credentials/InventoryApi.credentials.js"],
    "nodes": ["dist/nodes/Inventory/Inventory.node.js"]
  }
}
```

That `n8n-community-node-package` keyword is mandatory — it is how n8n's installer identifies valid packages on npm.

##### The credentials type

Credentials live in their own class, and this separation is what makes authentication secure and reusable.

```typescript
import {
  IAuthenticateGeneric,
  ICredentialTestRequest,
  ICredentialType,
  INodeProperties,
} from 'n8n-workflow';

export class InventoryApi implements ICredentialType {
  name = 'inventoryApi';
  displayName = 'Inventory API';
  documentationUrl = 'https://docs.inventory-vendor.com/authentication';

  properties: INodeProperties[] = [
    {
      displayName: 'API Key',
      name: 'apiKey',
      type: 'string',
      typeOptions: { password: true },   // masked in UI, encrypted at rest
      default: '',
      required: true,
    },
    {
      displayName: 'Base URL',
      name: 'baseUrl',
      type: 'string',
      default: 'https://api.inventory-vendor.com/v3',
      description: 'Override for self-hosted or sandbox instances.',
    },
  ];

  // Injected into every request automatically — the node never touches the secret
  authenticate: IAuthenticateGeneric = {
    type: 'generic',
    properties: {
      headers: { Authorization: '=Bearer {{$credentials.apiKey}}' },
    },
  };

  // Powers the "Test" button when a user saves the credential
  test: ICredentialTestRequest = {
    request: {
      baseURL: '={{$credentials.baseUrl}}',
      url: '/me',
    },
  };
}
```

Three properties make this the secure pattern. `typeOptions: { password: true }` masks the value in the UI. n8n **encrypts credentials at rest** with the instance encryption key, and they are referenced by name in exported workflow JSON rather than embedded — so sharing a workflow never leaks a secret. And because `authenticate` injects the header, **your node code never handles the credential value**, which means you cannot accidentally log it.

The `test` request is a small addition with real value: users find out immediately that a key is wrong, rather than at the first failed execution.

##### A declarative node

```typescript
import { INodeType, INodeTypeDescription, NodeConnectionType } from 'n8n-workflow';

export class Inventory implements INodeType {
  description: INodeTypeDescription = {
    displayName: 'Inventory',
    name: 'inventory',
    icon: 'file:inventory.svg',
    group: ['transform'],
    version: 1,
    subtitle: '={{$parameter["operation"]}}: {{$parameter["resource"]}}',
    description: 'Read and manage stock in the Inventory service',
    defaults: { name: 'Inventory' },
    inputs: [NodeConnectionType.Main],
    outputs: [NodeConnectionType.Main],
    credentials: [{ name: 'inventoryApi', required: true }],

    requestDefaults: {
      baseURL: '={{$credentials.baseUrl}}',
      headers: { Accept: 'application/json' },
    },

    properties: [
      {
        displayName: 'Resource',
        name: 'resource',
        type: 'options',
        noDataExpression: true,
        options: [{ name: 'Stock Item', value: 'stockItem' }],
        default: 'stockItem',
      },
      {
        displayName: 'Operation',
        name: 'operation',
        type: 'options',
        noDataExpression: true,
        displayOptions: { show: { resource: ['stockItem'] } },
        options: [
          {
            name: 'Get',
            value: 'get',
            action: 'Get a stock item',
            routing: { request: { method: 'GET', url: '=/stock/{{$parameter.sku}}' } },
          },
          {
            name: 'Create',
            value: 'create',
            action: 'Create a stock item',
            routing: {
              request: {
                method: 'POST',
                url: '/stock',
                body: {
                  sku: '={{$parameter.sku}}',
                  quantity: '={{$parameter.quantity}}',
                  warehouse_id: '={{$parameter.warehouseId}}',
                },
              },
              // unwrap the API's envelope so users get clean output
              output: { postReceive: [{ type: 'rootProperty', properties: { property: 'data' } }] },
            },
          },
        ],
        default: 'get',
      },
      {
        displayName: 'SKU',
        name: 'sku',
        type: 'string',
        required: true,
        default: '',
        displayOptions: { show: { resource: ['stockItem'] } },
      },
      {
        displayName: 'Quantity',
        name: 'quantity',
        type: 'number',
        default: 0,
        displayOptions: { show: { operation: ['create'] } },
      },
      {
        displayName: 'Warehouse Name or ID',
        name: 'warehouseId',
        type: 'options',
        typeOptions: { loadOptionsMethod: 'getWarehouses' },   // dynamic dropdown
        default: '',
        displayOptions: { show: { operation: ['create'] } },
      },
    ],
  };

  methods = {
    loadOptions: {
      async getWarehouses(this: any) {
        const res = await this.helpers.httpRequestWithAuthentication.call(
          this, 'inventoryApi',
          { method: 'GET', url: '/warehouses', json: true },
        );
        return res.data.map((w: any) => ({
          name: `${w.name} (${w.city})`,
          value: w.id,
        }));
      },
    },
  };
}
```

Details that make this feel native rather than homemade: `displayOptions.show` conditionally reveals fields so users only see what is relevant; `subtitle` shows the selected operation on the canvas; `action` strings populate n8n's searchable actions panel; `postReceive` unwraps the API envelope; and the `loadOptions` method turns an opaque warehouse ID into a picker of the user's own warehouses.

Note the field is named "Warehouse Name or ID" — an n8n convention that documents to users that an expression can supply either.

##### Programmatic nodes and error handling

When declarative is insufficient, `execute()` gives you the loop:

```typescript
async execute(this: IExecuteFunctions): Promise<INodeExecutionData[][]> {
  const items = this.getInputData();
  const out: INodeExecutionData[] = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
    try {
      const sku = this.getNodeParameter('sku', i) as string;

      const response = await this.helpers.httpRequestWithAuthentication.call(
        this, 'inventoryApi',
        { method: 'GET', url: `/stock/${encodeURIComponent(sku)}`, json: true },
      );

      out.push({
        json: response.data,
        pairedItem: { item: i },          // preserves lineage for downstream nodes
      });
    } catch (error) {
      // Respect the user's "Continue On Fail" setting
      if (this.continueOnFail()) {
        out.push({
          json: { error: (error as Error).message },
          pairedItem: { item: i },
        });
        continue;
      }
      throw new NodeApiError(this.getNode(), error as JsonObject, { itemIndex: i });
    }
  }

  return [out];
}
```

Three obligations of a well-behaved node appear there. **Iterate over input items** — a node receiving 50 items must handle all 50. **Set `pairedItem`** so n8n can trace which output came from which input, which powers the data-lineage view users rely on when debugging. And **honour `continueOnFail()`** rather than always throwing, plus wrap errors in `NodeApiError`/`NodeOperationError` with an `itemIndex`, so the UI shows a useful message pointing at the offending item.

##### Local testing and publishing

```bash
npm run build            # TypeScript -> dist/
npm link                 # register the package globally

cd ~/.n8n/custom || (mkdir -p ~/.n8n/custom && cd ~/.n8n/custom)
npm init -y
npm link n8n-nodes-inventory

n8n start                # your node appears in the palette
```

For iteration, `npm run dev` (TypeScript watch mode) plus restarting n8n gives a workable loop. Self-hosted instances can also install community nodes through **Settings → Community Nodes** by npm package name — set `N8N_COMMUNITY_PACKAGES_ENABLED=true`.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Clone starter] --> B[Define credentials<br/>type]
    B --> C[Describe node<br/>declarative routing]
    C --> D[npm run build<br/>+ npm link]
    D --> E[Test in local n8n]
    E --> F{Works?}
    F -->|no| C
    F -->|yes| G[npm publish<br/>keyword: community-node]
    G --> H[Install via<br/>Community Nodes]
```

Publishing to npm requires the `n8n-community-node-package` keyword, a clear README with credential setup instructions, an appropriate licence, and semantic versioning. Submitting to n8n's verified list adds a review covering security, error handling and UX conventions.

##### Common pitfalls

- **Only processing `items[0]`** instead of iterating every input item.
- **Missing `pairedItem`**, breaking data lineage in the UI.
- **Ignoring `continueOnFail()`**, so one bad item kills a 500-item batch.
- **Throwing raw errors** rather than `NodeApiError` with an item index.
- **Handling credential values in node code** instead of letting `authenticate` inject them.
- **No `test` request** on the credential, deferring auth failures to run time.
- **Forgetting `displayOptions`**, showing users irrelevant fields.
- **Not rebuilding** after edits — n8n loads `dist/`, not your TypeScript.
- **Breaking changes without bumping the node `version`**, which n8n supports precisely so old workflows keep working.
- **No pagination** in list operations, silently returning page one.

##### Your hands-on tie-in

The practical task: scaffold from the starter, implement a **declarative node for a public API with one credential type and two operations** (get and create), run it in a local n8n, and use it in a workflow. Choose an API with a simple key-in-header auth for the first attempt. The moment your node appears in the node palette with its own icon and a working credential test is genuinely satisfying, and it makes the plugin architecture concrete.

The mini project is a publishable package: a custom node with a **credentials type, multiple operations, a `loadOptions` dynamic dropdown, and clean error handling**, built, tested and packaged as an installable npm community node. Test the error path deliberately — pass an invalid SKU with "Continue On Fail" both on and off, and confirm the two behaviours differ correctly.

For the assignment, compare **declarative and programmatic node styles**, explaining when each is appropriate, and describe **how the credentials type keeps authentication secure and reusable**. The security argument has three parts worth stating explicitly: encryption at rest with the instance key, reference-by-name in exported workflows so secrets never travel with shared JSON, and header injection that keeps the secret out of your node's code path entirely.

##### Key takeaways

- Custom nodes replace copy-pasted HTTP requests with one versioned, maintainable component — and give colleagues a native dropdown experience instead of raw API construction.
- Prefer declarative `routing` for standard REST APIs; use programmatic `execute()` only for chained requests, custom pagination, binary data or non-HTTP protocols.
- The credentials class is the security boundary: masked, encrypted at rest, referenced by name in exports, injected into requests so node code never sees the secret.
- Well-behaved nodes iterate all input items, set `pairedItem`, honour `continueOnFail()`, and throw `NodeApiError` with an item index.
- Use `displayOptions`, `loadOptions` dropdowns, `subtitle` and `action` labels to make the node feel native, and version it properly so existing workflows keep working.
